The paper’s main theorem is existential and asymptotic: for fixed \(k\ge3\), sufficiently large equal parts and minimum legal co-degree at least \(n/k\) guarantee a matching of size \(n-1\). Its proof splits into extremal and non-extremal cases and uses a randomized absorbing matching; it does not state a finite \(n_0\) or an efficient certificate-extraction algorithm. I’m therefore not treating “the theorem guarantees a matching” as a hard generator by itself—the obvious dense planted-matching distribution would be easy for matching search. I’m checking whether the paper’s absorbing-edge lemma supports an honest Track B family with a short exact witness and a large mechanical/compact-route gap.
