   474	We claim that $f(k)\ge 0$. To see this, we first note that
   475	$f(1)=\Delta_j> 0$ (as $\Delta_j\ge 0$ and $\Delta_j\ne 0$). Hence, since $f(1)\equiv 0 \pmod k$, 
   476	$f(1)\ge k$. This implies $f(2)=f(1)-k\ge 0$; so $f(2)\ge k$ as $f(2)\ne 0$. Continuing this process and using the assumption 
   477	that $f(l)\ne 0$ for $l\in [k-1]$, we conclude that $f(k)\ge 0$. 
