+# Almost perfect matchings in k-partite k-graphs — verified generator
+
+| Profile | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | linear algebra |
+| Certificate | matrix certificate (three fixed-width binary row strings) |
+| Intended intuition | change of variables |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | none |
+
+Status: all local gates pass, but the mandatory multi-vendor oracle run is **not
+complete**. OpenRouter rejected every bare, structural-hint, and placebo call
+with HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` before a scored attempt. The
+module therefore has no hardened claim yet; `hard` is the provisional shipping
+preset. The error records are retained in the three required transcript files.
+
+## Problem and trust boundary
+
+This generator uses the native absorbing-edge object from Lu, Wang, and Yu,
+[“Almost perfect matchings in k-partite k-graphs”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.04838).
+Section 3 defines a type-
+\(j\) set \(S\) and an \(S\)-absorbing edge: one matching edge can be replaced
+by two edges that also cover \(S\). Here \(k=3\), every part is
+\(\mathbb F_2^d\), and an exact binary syndrome predicate defines the
+hyperedges. The solver returns one edge; `verify` checks its shape,
+disjointness, membership, and both alternatives in the paper’s definition
+using only exact GF(2) operations.
+
+The certificate is known by composition of identities. The generator samples
+\(S\), applies the three displayed coordinate changes, and XORs transformed
+vectors so that the candidate and two replacement triples all have the zero
+syndrome. Zero is planted in the target-syndrome set. No matching or absorber
+search is run during generation. Every legal pair has exactly
+\(|T|2^{d-r}\) neighbors, so the positive co-degree condition used by Lemma
+3.2 holds exactly.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+This is not a Track A or average-case hardness claim. Generic GF(2) Gaussian
+elimination followed by three syndrome solves finds an absorber in
+\(O(r^2d)\) bit operations. At `hard`, the final local eight-instance run
+measured 16,552 bit operations and about 0.002 seconds per instance; the exact
+wall time for the latest run is in `selftest_report.json`. The compact route notices that all three part maps feed one common
+syndrome, changes coordinates, and performs exactly \(3d=240\) XORs. That is
+the intended mechanical-versus-structural gap.
+
+The paper’s main Theorem 1.1 is stronger and different: for fixed \(k\ge3\),
+sufficiently large equal parts and minimum legal co-degree at least \(n/k\)
+guarantee a matching of size \(n-1\). It gives no explicit finite \(n_0\).
+Its extremal proof uses maximum matchings, while its non-extremal proof uses
+the probabilistic absorbing Lemma 3.4. A dense planted almost-perfect matching
+would have many easy witnesses, so this module does not make that false Track A
+claim. Lemma 3.2 also explains the easy direction here: larger co-degree creates
+many absorbers, so the target fraction is kept small and fixed.
+
+## Worked demo (`seed=0`)
+
+This smallest instance is hand-solvable. Coordinate 0 is printed first.
+
+```text
+Find an S-absorbing edge in the following 3-partite 3-uniform hypergraph.
+
+GF(2)^d has d=4.  For P, (Pv)[j]=v[P[j]].
+An edge satisfies A(P1 x XOR P2 y XOR P3 z) in T.
+
+P1 = [0,1,2,3]
+P2 = [3,0,1,2]
+P3 = [2,3,0,1]
+
+Rows of A:
+0111
+1011
+
+T:
+00
+10
+
+a  = 1110
+a' = 0010
+b  = 0111
+c  = 0100
+
+Find a disjoint edge (x,y,z) such that either
+(a',y,c),(a,b,z) are edges or (a,y,c),(a',b,z) are edges.
+```
+
+Answer:
+
+```text
+<answer>["0110","0110","0101"]</answer>
+```
+
+`verify(inst, inst["answer"])` returns `(True, "ok")`. Dropping the final row
+returns `(False, "answer is missing a part vector")`.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | d | rank r | target count | vertices per part | Intended XORs | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 4 | 2 | 2 | 16 | 12 | hand example |
+| easy | 28 | 11 | 6 | \(2^{28}\) | 84 | oracle unscored |
+| medium | 52 | 15 | 18 | \(2^{52}\) | 156 | not reached |
+| hard | 80 | 16 | 18 | \(2^{80}\) | 240 | provisional shipping |
+
+Escalation first raises syndrome rank at fixed `d`, shrinking the allowed-edge
+fraction without lengthening the witness. Only after that fixed-length axis is
+exhausted does it use the remaining answer-size allowance.
