@@ -0,0 +1,181 @@
+# Almost perfect matchings in k-partite k-graphs — verified generator
+
+| Profile | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | linear algebra |
+| Certificate | matrix certificate (three fixed-width binary row strings) |
+| Intended intuition | change of variables |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | none |
+
+Status: all local gates pass, but the mandatory multi-vendor oracle run is **not
+complete**. OpenRouter rejected every bare, structural-hint, and placebo call
+with HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` before a scored attempt. The
+module therefore has no hardened claim yet; `hard` is the provisional shipping
+preset. The error records are retained in the three required transcript files.
+
+## Problem and trust boundary
+
+This generator uses the native absorbing-edge object from Lu, Wang, and Yu,
+[“Almost perfect matchings in k-partite k-graphs”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.04838).
+Section 3 defines a type-
+\(j\) set \(S\) and an \(S\)-absorbing edge: one matching edge can be replaced
+by two edges that also cover \(S\). Here \(k=3\), every part is
+\(\mathbb F_2^d\), and an exact binary syndrome predicate defines the
+hyperedges. The solver returns one edge; `verify` checks its shape,
+disjointness, membership, and both alternatives in the paper’s definition
+using only exact GF(2) operations.
+
+The certificate is known by composition of identities. The generator samples
+\(S\), applies the three displayed coordinate changes, and XORs transformed
+vectors so that the candidate and two replacement triples all have the zero
+syndrome. Zero is planted in the target-syndrome set. No matching or absorber
+search is run during generation. Every legal pair has exactly
+\(|T|2^{d-r}\) neighbors, so the positive co-degree condition used by Lemma
+3.2 holds exactly.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+This is not a Track A or average-case hardness claim. Generic GF(2) Gaussian
+elimination followed by three syndrome solves finds an absorber in
+\(O(r^2d)\) bit operations. At `hard`, the final local eight-instance run
+measured 16,552 bit operations and 0.002–0.049 seconds per instance across
+repeated local runs; the latest exact wall time is in `selftest_report.json`.
+The compact route notices that all three part maps feed one common
+syndrome, changes coordinates, and performs exactly \(3d=240\) XORs. That is
+the intended mechanical-versus-structural gap.
+
+The paper’s main Theorem 1.1 is stronger and different: for fixed \(k\ge3\),
+sufficiently large equal parts and minimum legal co-degree at least \(n/k\)
+guarantee a matching of size \(n-1\). It gives no explicit finite \(n_0\).
+Its extremal proof uses maximum matchings, while its non-extremal proof uses
+the probabilistic absorbing Lemma 3.4. A dense planted almost-perfect matching
+would have many easy witnesses, so this module does not make that false Track A
+claim. Lemma 3.2 also explains the easy direction here: larger co-degree creates
+many absorbers, so the target fraction is kept small and fixed.
+
+## Worked demo (`seed=0`)
+
+This smallest instance is hand-solvable. Coordinate 0 is printed first.
+
+```text
+Find an S-absorbing edge in the following 3-partite 3-uniform hypergraph.
+
+GF(2)^d has d=4.  For P, (Pv)[j]=v[P[j]].
+An edge satisfies A(P1 x XOR P2 y XOR P3 z) in T.
+
+P1 = [0,1,2,3]
+P2 = [3,0,1,2]
+P3 = [2,3,0,1]
+
+Rows of A:
+0111
+1011
+
+T:
+00
+10
+
+a  = 1110
+a' = 0010
+b  = 0111
+c  = 0100
+
+Find a disjoint edge (x,y,z) such that either
+(a',y,c),(a,b,z) are edges or (a,y,c),(a',b,z) are edges.
+```
+
+Answer:
+
+```text
+<answer>["0110","0110","0101"]</answer>
+```
+
+`verify(inst, inst["answer"])` returns `(True, "ok")`. Dropping the final row
+returns `(False, "answer is missing a part vector")`.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | d | rank r | target count | vertices per part | Intended XORs | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 4 | 2 | 2 | 16 | 12 | hand example |
+| easy | 28 | 11 | 6 | \(2^{28}\) | 84 | oracle unscored |
+| medium | 52 | 15 | 18 | \(2^{52}\) | 156 | not reached |
+| hard | 80 | 16 | 18 | \(2^{80}\) | 240 | provisional shipping |
+
+Escalation first raises syndrome rank at fixed `d`, shrinking the allowed-edge
+fraction without lengthening the witness. Only after that fixed-length axis is
+exhausted does it use the remaining answer-size allowance.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| Gate | Result | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 preset–seed plants verified |
+| G2 | pass | five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged fenced JSON round-tripped; garbage returned `None` |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 uniform **S-disjoint edge** guesses verified |
+| G5 | pass | demo has exactly 512 witnesses; reference algorithm 8/8; hard candidate space is about \(4.85\times10^{68}\) |
+| G6 | pass | minimum-weight, edge-first greedy, 256 restarts, and raw-coordinate XOR each succeeded 0/8 |
+| G7 | pass | dimension 160 built and its planted witness verified |
+| G8 | pass | 80/80 invariant keys, 80/80 carried witnesses, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 250 characters, 63 estimated tokens, three strings/240 explicit bits, 240 XORs |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostic
+
+| Run | Preset | Scored | Result |
+|---|---|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0/3 | four redraws, all HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
+| structural hint | hard | 0/3 | four redraws, all HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
+| placebo hint | hard | 0/3 | four redraws, all HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
+
+Thus bare/hinted/placebo are each 0 solved out of **0 scored attempts**;
+hinted-minus-placebo is undefined, and no conclusion about the claimed change-
+of-variables intuition is justified. Rerun all three arms after restoring the
+OpenRouter allowance. The hint only names the shared-syndrome invariant; it
+does not provide the XOR construction.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import importlib.util, json
+
+spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("generator", "results/1608.04838/gen_1608_04838.py")
+g = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
+spec.loader.exec_module(g)
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+statement = g.render(inst)
+answer = g.parse_answer(f"<answer>{json.dumps(inst['answer'])}</answer>")
+assert g.verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit after the oracle blocker is cleared:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 1608.04838
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+- The successful Gaussian reference method is intentionally disclosed; this
+  distribution is efficiently solvable with tools. It benchmarks finding and
+  executing the shorter invariant route in a no-tool context.
+- The succinct binary predicate represents an enormous hypergraph without
+  listing its edges. It preserves the paper’s exact hypergraph and absorbing
+  definitions, but it is a specially algebraic subfamily, not a benchmark for
+  arbitrary dense hypergraphs or for the full almost-perfect-matching theorem.
+- The 0/200,000 figure samples the strongest obvious prior—uniform disjoint
+  hyperedges—not arbitrary triples. It is an observed fraction, not a
+  confidence-level proof that the true density is zero.
+- The panel did not test a SAT/ILP encoding, quantum search, or learned attacks.
+  Gaussian elimination dominates the natural exact attack for this encoding.
+- Full succinct-hypergraph isomorphism is not canonicalized. The key covers
+  coordinate relabeling, syndrome-basis changes, admissible translations,
+  part/name swaps, target order, and their tested compositions.
+- Most importantly, no multi-vendor oracle attempt was scored because the
+  configured OpenRouter key was exhausted. Trust G/V and the measured Track B
+  cost; do not trust a claim that the current `hard` preset defeats the pool
+  until the transcripts are regenerated successfully.
