-## Problem and trust boundary
+## What the problem is, and what is verified
 
-This generator uses the native absorbing-edge object from Lu, Wang, and Yu,
-[“Almost perfect matchings in k-partite k-graphs”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.04838).
-Section 3 defines a type-
-\(j\) set \(S\) and an \(S\)-absorbing edge: one matching edge can be replaced
-by two edges that also cover \(S\). Here \(k=3\), every part is
-\(\mathbb F_2^d\), and an exact binary syndrome predicate defines the
-hyperedges. The solver returns one edge; `verify` checks its shape,
-disjointness, membership, and both alternatives in the paper’s definition
-using only exact GF(2) operations.
+This generator uses the exact *type-j set* and *S-absorbing edge* definition from Section 3 of Lu, Wang, and Yu, [“Almost perfect matchings in k-partite k-graphs”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.04838). It specializes to `k=3`. Each of the three labeled vertex parts is `GF(2)^d`; a displayed full-rank binary matrix, three coordinate permutations, and a target syndrome set define the hyperedges. The solver must return one edge disjoint from `S` that can be replaced by two edges covering `S`. `verify` checks dimensions, disjointness, the candidate edge, and both possible replacement pairs by exact GF(2) arithmetic. It never reads `inst["answer"]`.
 
-The certificate is known by composition of identities. The generator samples
-\(S\), applies the three displayed coordinate changes, and XORs transformed
-vectors so that the candidate and two replacement triples all have the zero
-syndrome. Zero is planted in the target-syndrome set. No matching or absorber
-search is run during generation. Every legal pair has exactly
-\(|T|2^{d-r}\) neighbors, so the positive co-degree condition used by Lemma
-3.2 holds exactly.
+Generation is by composition of identities, not search. If `q` is the XOR of the target syndromes, construction makes `q=A(P1(a xor a'))`. Three cross-part XORs then give the planted edge and both replacement edges syndrome `q`. The target set is composed around `q`; the planted syndrome is no longer the zero-syndrome outlier present in an earlier draft.
 
-## Why Track B
+## Why this is Track B
 
-This is not a Track A or average-case hardness claim. Generic GF(2) Gaussian
-elimination followed by three syndrome solves finds an absorber in
-\(O(r^2d)\) bit operations. At `hard`, the final local eight-instance run
-measured 16,552 bit operations and 0.002–0.049 seconds per instance across
-repeated local runs; the latest exact wall time is in `selftest_report.json`.
-The compact route notices that all three part maps feed one common
-syndrome, changes coordinates, and performs exactly \(3d=240\) XORs. That is
-the intended mechanical-versus-structural gap.
+The paper proves an existential co-degree theorem, not average-case hardness. Section 3, Lemma 3.2 shows that suitable co-degrees create many absorbing edges, and Lemma 3.4 obtains a small absorbing matching probabilistically. Therefore this module makes its efficient algorithm explicit: row reduction and three syndrome solves over GF(2), complexity `O(r^2 d)`. On eight hard instances it solved 8/8 and averaged **18,344 counted bit operations** (**0.0017--0.0032 s per instance** across repeated local runs). The compact route is **240 coordinate XORs** after recognizing the shared-syndrome invariant. The intended difficulty is finding that compression without a sandbox, not computational hardness.
 
-The paper’s main Theorem 1.1 is stronger and different: for fixed \(k\ge3\),
-sufficiently large equal parts and minimum legal co-degree at least \(n/k\)
-guarantee a matching of size \(n-1\). It gives no explicit finite \(n_0\).
-Its extremal proof uses maximum matchings, while its non-extremal proof uses
-the probabilistic absorbing Lemma 3.4. A dense planted almost-perfect matching
-would have many easy witnesses, so this module does not make that false Track A
-claim. Lemma 3.2 also explains the easy direction here: larger co-degree creates
-many absorbers, so the target fraction is kept small and fixed.
+Theorem 1.1 guarantees a matching of size `n-1` when every part has sufficiently large size and minimum legal co-degree at least `n/k`. This generator does **not** claim to pose that whole matching problem: its witness is the local absorber used in the non-extremal proof. At the hard preset its co-degree fraction is `17/65536`, deliberately small enough that a structure-aware random edge is rarely absorbing.
 
